    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d630">

                <pb facs="0945" n="825" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg825"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/4544</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-06-07"><hi rendition="#italic">June 7,
                        1922</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received July 11(?).]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 707</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to enclose copies of
                        correspondence<anchor n="98" xml:id="d630-f1"/> from the American Consul,
                    Tsinan, Province of Shantung, relative to the recent declaration of martial law
                    by the Chinese authorities at Tsinan and the territory along the Tientsin–Pukow
                    and Shantung Railways. There is also enclosed a translation of the Martial Law
                    Rules together with the comments of the Consular Body at Tsinan thereon.<anchor
                        n="98" xml:id="d630-f2"/> It will be noted that there was some divergence of
                    view among the members of the Consular Body as to what extent these rules were
                    applicable to foreigners. The Legation in its instruction to the Consul at
                    Tsinan dated May 25th, copy of which is enclosed,<anchor n="98" xml:id="d630-f3"
                    /> approved the position taken by Mr. Gauss in his despatch to the Commissioner
                    for Foreign Affairs dated May 2, 1922, in which he stated that while he could
                    not consent to the application of martial law to American citizens in impairment
                    of treaty rights, he was disposed to give all proper assistance to the
                    authorities in maintaining order and in preventing or suppressing any possible
                    participation by Americans in any improper or unlawful acts. A copy of this
                    despatch is transmitted as an enclosure to Mr. Gauss’ despatch No. 327, dated
                    May 2, 1922.<anchor n="98" xml:id="d630-f4"/></p>
                <p>The Legation would appreciate an expression of the views of the Department on the
                    general subject of the applicability of martial law to persons possessed of
                    extraterritorial privileges for the guidance of the various Consular officers in
                    China.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jacob Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
